A||||5||wwh0001||Valve Cover Question||||5||
Z||000000||wwh0001||12-10-1999||08:35 AM||||I have an 87 GT with the 2.8 which was recently rebuilt. Now the right valve cover gasket is leaking. The person that did the rebuild lives a long way from me. I would prefer to replace the gasket if I can rather than taking it back. My question is can the valve cover on the right side ( cover closest to the trunk) be removed without removing the plenum? All of the bolts are easily accessable, but I can't tell if there is enough room to lift the cover off.<BR>Thanks for taking the time to read this. I have followed this forum for some time and there is obvioulsy a lot of Fiero knowlege on this board.||||reg||5
Z||000001||dogtired||12-10-1999||10:30 AM||||You have a somewhat controversial subject here.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Some folks on the lists say it can be done.<BR>Personally. I Don't see how?<BR>Perhaps that is why you are having your leak. The trunk side (right) would be easier than the firewall side but still you have to make sure there is some sealer where the head meets the block (the crack) and make sure the rubber gasket is in place. Which is tough to do with everything in the way.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> I have done it to 2 cars V6's by removing the plenums, (good time to check vacuum lines and replace the O ring in distributor) it is time consuming but not hard, except on your back. Some folks even say to stand in your trunk, but I am too big for that, so bend the back. I recommend only using rubber gaskets, the blue Felpro look kind of cool under the red valve cover. Cork is harder to keep in place and will dry out and shrink causing leaks later.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Just my thoughts.||||reg||6
Z||000002||Carrolles||12-10-1999||10:42 AM||||I agre with Dogtired.  I just put in a new motor and when I had it out of the car I could not see a way to access all of the valve cover bolts much less get the cover off without taking off the plenum.  You will have to lift the cover enough to clear he rocker arms and I don't think there is enough room to do that.||||reg||1
Z||000003||wwh0001||12-10-1999||11:18 AM||||Thanks for the replys. I know I can get the bolts off, but I was concerned the valve cover would not come off due to the clearance with the plenum. <BR>looks like the job will be a bit more than I had hoped.<BR>thanks again||||reg||8
Z||000004||Gokart||12-10-1999||08:42 PM||||I'm have a small leak in that area also, so I'll be replacing the gasket soon as well, but I don't think taking off the plenum is a big deal/job anyway.......right!?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Linx||12-12-1999||12:19 AM||||Not really.  I've done it four times in the last two months.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I timed myself and I got 'em off in about an hour.  I can do it in my sleep now.  It takes longer to put it all back on- thanks to the chore of scrapin' the old gasket material off.<P>The valve cover gaskets are rubber.  Very very thick rubber and cost $20 a set.  They're specially made for that valve cover- look closely and you'll see they actually close around the cover bolts and protrude upwards thru the cover holes somewhat.  I cant imagine using cork here.<P>As an aside:  The first time I disassembled the engine, I pulled off the gaskets and thought they were RTV; I have no idea where my brain was.  I pitched them in the trash.  Days later, i realized I couldnt make RTV work.  Then it occured to me and cha-ching; Advance Auto & $20.<P>And Dog's right, this is a great time to check on those vacuum lines.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 12-14-1999).]||||reg||1
